We use the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion to find the number of boxes that contain either pens or pencils. The Principle says that

\begin{align*}
\text{number of boxes that contain pens or pencils} &= \text{number of boxes that contain pens} + \\
&\quad\text{number of boxes that contain pencils} - \\
&\quad\text{number of boxes that contain both pens and pencils}
\end{align*}From this, we find that there are $5 + 4 - 2 = 7$ boxes that contain pens or pencils. Since there are a total of 10 boxes, the remaining boxes must contain neither pens nor pencils, so there are $10-7 = \boxed{3}$ boxes that contain neither pens nor pencils.

